NNAAMMEE
     llppttccoonnttrrooll — a utility for manipulating the lpt printer driver

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llppttccoonnttrrooll --ee | --ii | --pp | --ss [--dd _c_o_n_t_r_o_l___d_e_v_i_c_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The llppttccoonnttrrooll utility is used to set either the interrupt-driven, ex‐
     tended, standard, or polling mode of individual lpt(4) devices.  When a
     printer is switched from a mode to another, this change will only take
     effect the next time the device is opened.

     Extended mode is anything the parallel port interface can support.  For
     an ECP/ISA parallel port, it may be FIFO+DMA or ECP.

     The following command line options are supported:

     --ee      Turn on extended mode.

     --ii      Turn on interrupt-driven mode.

     --pp      Turn on polled mode.

     --ss      Turn on standard mode, i.e., turn off extended mode.

     --dd _c_o_n_t_r_o_l___d_e_v_i_c_e
             Set the mode of the printer control device specified by
             _c_o_n_t_r_o_l___d_e_v_i_c_e.  The default value for _c_o_n_t_r_o_l___d_e_v_i_c_e is
             _/_d_e_v_/_l_p_t_0_._c_t_l.

     One of --ee, --ii, --pp or --ss must be specified.

FFIILLEESS
     /dev/lpt?               printer devices
     /dev/lpt?.ctl           printer control devices
     /sys/i386/conf/GENERIC  kernel configuration file
     /boot/device.hints      device hints for the parallel port chipset
                             driver, ppc(4)

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     lpt(4), ppc(4), device.hints(5)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The llppttccoonnttrrooll utility first appeared in FreeBSD 1.1.5

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     Geoffrey M. Rehmet

BBUUGGSS
     The control device name should never have been an option, but should have
     been an optional argument.  Because of this, a single argument is treated
     as a device name.

